.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt NOQTIS-CAPSULE 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm noqtis-capsule
.Nd the noqtis capsule format, version 1
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A capsule is a folder that holds the files of a result together with
.Pa noqtis.json ,
the manifest, and
.Pa noqtis.sig ,
the seal, both at the top of the folder.
The manifest lists every file with its size and two digests; the seal
signs the manifest's exact bytes with a post-quantum signature.
.Pp
The key words MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD and MAY are to be read as in
RFC 2119 and RFC 8174.
.Ss Canonical JSON
Both files MUST be JSON (RFC 8259) in the canonical form of RFC 8785
(JCS): UTF-8, no whitespace outside strings, object members sorted by the
UTF-16 code units of their names, strings with the minimal escapes, and
numbers in the shortest form that round-trips.
A checker MUST reject a file that is not byte-identical to its own
canonical form, has a duplicate member name, or is not valid UTF-8.
.Ss The manifest, noqtis.json
An object with exactly these members:
.Bl -tag -width "created"
.It Cm created
A string: the creation time, RFC 3339 in UTC,
.Ql YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ
with optional fractional seconds before the
.Ql Z .
.It Cm files
An array with one object per file, sorted by the bytes of
.Cm path ,
with no path twice.
Each object has exactly:
.Bl -tag -width "sha3_256"
.It Cm path
the file's path relative to the capsule folder, a string;
.It Cm sha256
its SHA-256, 64 lower-case hex digits;
.It Cm sha3_256
its SHA3-256, 64 lower-case hex digits;
.It Cm size
its size in bytes, an integer from 0 to 2^53\-1.
.El
.It Cm format
The string
.Ql noqtis-capsule-1 .
.It Cm meta
Optional: any JSON object, at most 1 MiB, opaque to noqtis.
.El
.Pp
Because of the canonical order, the manifest always reads
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"created":...,"files":[{"path":...,"sha256":...,
"sha3_256":...,"size":...},...],"format":"noqtis-capsule-1"
[,"meta":{...}]}
.Ed
.Ss Paths
A path MUST use
.Ql /
between names, MUST NOT start with
.Ql / ,
and MUST be in normal form: no empty segment and no
.Ql \&. .
Every name (segment) MUST be valid UTF-8 without overlong forms or
surrogates, MUST NOT be empty,
.Ql \&.
or
.Ql .. ,
and MUST NOT contain a control character (U+0000 to U+001F, U+007F),
.Ql / ,
.Ql \e
or
.Ql \&: .
Two paths of one capsule MUST NOT differ only in ASCII case.
A path MUST be at most 4096 bytes.
.Pp
.Pa noqtis.json
and
.Pa noqtis.sig
at the top of the folder are not listed.
Only regular files are listed; folders are implied by the paths, so an
empty folder is not recorded.
.Ss The seal, noqtis.sig
An object with exactly these members:
.Bl -tag -width "manifest_sha256"
.It Cm alg
The signature algorithm, as
.Fn nqsname
.Pq Xr nqsign 3
names it, for example
.Ql ML-DSA-65
or
.Ql SLH-DSA-SHAKE-128s .
.It Cm context
The FIPS 204/205 context string the signature was made with: exactly
.Ql noqtis-capsule-1 .
A checker refuses any other context, so that a signature made for
another purpose (a plain
.Xr noqtis-sign 1
of the same bytes, say) is never taken for a seal.
.It Cm format
The string
.Ql noqtis-seal-1 .
.It Cm manifest_sha256
The SHA-256 of the exact bytes of
.Pa noqtis.json ,
64 lower-case hex digits.
.It Cm public_key
The signer's public key, base64 (RFC 4648 section 4, padded, no line
breaks) of the standard's encoding.
.It Cm signature
The pure ML-DSA or SLH-DSA signature over the exact bytes of
.Pa noqtis.json
with that context, base64 likewise.
.It Cm timestamp
Optional: base64 of an RFC 3161 time-stamp token whose message imprint is
the hash (SHA-256, SHA-384 or SHA-512) of the decoded
.Cm signature
bytes.
.Xr noqtis-seal 1
.Fl -tsa
adds it
.Pq Xr nqtsput 3 .
.El
.Ss Limits
A checker MUST refuse, with a failing check and never by truncating:
.Pa noqtis.json
over 512 MiB (its size is checked before it is read, and again if it
grows while being read);
.Pa noqtis.sig
over 1 MiB;
.Cm meta
over 1 MiB; more than 2,000,000 files; a path over 4096 bytes; folders
more than 64 levels deep.
JSON nesting is limited to 64 levels.
.Ss Sealing
A sealer MUST list every entry of the folder without following links and
MUST refuse to seal a folder that holds a symbolic link, a Windows
reparse point, a special file (device, pipe, socket), a name the rules
above refuse, two paths that differ only in ASCII case, or anything over
a limit.
It MUST verify the new signature before writing the seal.
.Ss Checking
A checker MUST:
.Bl -enum
.It
check that both files are canonical with exactly the fields above and
the right formats, and that
.Cm alg
is known and
.Cm public_key
and
.Cm signature
have the algorithm's sizes;
.It
check that the SHA-256 of
.Pa noqtis.json
equals
.Cm manifest_sha256 ;
.It
verify
.Cm signature
over the exact bytes of
.Pa noqtis.json
with
.Cm context ,
using the trusted public key when the caller gave one (and then require
that
.Cm public_key
equal it), otherwise
.Cm public_key ,
and say which;
.It
for every listed file: require it to exist, to be a regular file reached
without following a link, and to have the listed size, SHA-256 and
SHA3-256;
.It
fail on every regular file present but not listed, every link, special
file or refused name in the folder, every path out of order, listed
twice, not in normal form, or differing from another only in case;
.It
when trust anchors for time-stamping authorities are given, require a
.Cm timestamp
whose imprint matches and whose token verifies, with the TSA certificate
path valid at the token's genTime, under one of them; when none are given,
fail a
.Cm timestamp
that is present, since it cannot be checked.
.El
.Pp
A capsule passes only when every check passes.
.Ss Format 2
A format 2 capsule has the manifest format
.Ql noqtis-capsule-2
and the seal format
.Ql noqtis-seal-2 .
Its manifest is the format 1 manifest with these members after
.Cm format ,
in canonical order:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm inputs
Optional: 1 to 1024 objects
.Brq Cm capsule , name :
.Cm capsule
is the id of a capsule this one was made from, 64 lower-case hex
digits;
.Cm name
is 1 to 255 bytes of UTF-8 with no control characters.
.It Cm merkle
.Brq Cm alg , leaves , root :
.Cm alg
is
.Ql sha256 ,
.Cm leaves
the number of file entries and
.Cm root
the Merkle tree head, 64 lower-case hex digits.
.It Cm meta
Optional, as in format 1.
.El
.Pp
The tree is the Merkle Tree Hash of RFC 9162, section 2.1.1, over the
file entries in manifest order: each leaf is SHA-256 of a 0x00 byte and
the entry's canonical bytes as they appear in the manifest, each node
SHA-256 of a 0x01 byte and its two children; the tree of no entries is
SHA-256 of the empty string.
.Pp
The
.Em head
is the manifest with the member
.Ql \&"files":[...],
removed: the bytes before
.Ql \&"files"
and the bytes after the comma that ends the array.
The seal signs the head, not the manifest: it has
.Cm head_sha256 ,
the SHA-256 of the head, in place of
.Cm manifest_sha256 ,
and its
.Cm context
is
.Ql noqtis-capsule-2 .
A checker MUST, besides the checks of format 1 made over the head, check
that the tree over the listed entries has the head's
.Cm root
and
.Cm leaves .
.Pp
A capsule's
.Em id
is the SHA-256 of the bytes its seal signs: the manifest in format 1,
the head in format 2.
.Ss Proofs
A proof
.Pq Xr noqtis-prove 1
shows that some entries are in a format 2 capsule.
It is canonical JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"entries":[{"entry":E,"index":I,"path":[H,...]},...],
 "format":"noqtis-proof-1","head":HEAD,"seal":SEAL}
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm seal
is the seal object,
.Cm head
the head as a JSON object, and each entry
.Cm entry
a file entry exactly as in the manifest, at position
.Cm index
(from 0), with
.Cm path
its audit path (RFC 9162, section 2.1.3.1), each node 64 lower-case hex
digits, from the leaf up.
A checker MUST check the seal and its signature over the canonical bytes
of
.Cm head
as for a capsule, then each entry's path to the head's
.Cm root
with the tree size
.Cm leaves
by the algorithm of RFC 9162, section 2.1.3.2.
The tree size is authenticated by the signed head, not by the path.
A proof holds no name, size or digest of the entries it does not
prove.
.Ss Key statements
A key statement is canonical JSON with the members
.Cm alg , at , format , key , kind , reason
and
.Cm signature ,
and for a rotation
.Cm new_alg , new_key
and
.Cm new_signature :
.Cm format
is
.Ql noqtis-keys-1 ;
.Cm kind
is
.Ql rotate
or
.Ql revoke ;
.Cm alg
and
.Cm key
are the key's algorithm and base64 public key,
.Cm new_alg
and
.Cm new_key
those of the key that replaces it;
.Cm at
is a time as
.Ql YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ ;
.Cm reason
is up to 255 bytes of text, possibly empty.
.Cm signature
is by
.Cm key ,
and
.Cm new_signature
by
.Cm new_key ,
each with context
.Ql noqtis-keys-1
over the statement without its two signature members, in canonical
form.
Both algorithms are post-quantum.
.Pp
A checker given a trusted key and statements follows them: the trusted
key, the key a valid rotation of it names, and so on.
It trusts no key when two statements are about one key or a rotation
leads back to an earlier one.
A seal's key passes when it is in that chain.
When that key was rotated away or revoked at
.Cm at ,
the seal passes only with a verified timestamp whose genTime is earlier.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa noqtis.json
the manifest
.It Pa noqtis.sig
the seal
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A manifest (one line in the file; broken here for width):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"created":"2026-10-28T23:35:26Z","files":[{"path":"data/in.csv",
"sha256":"5be08c96...","sha3_256":"cec130cc...","size":4},
{"path":"report.txt","sha256":"8e54b0ca...","sha3_256":"cfd6402f...",
"size":2}],"format":"noqtis-capsule-1","meta":{"project":"demo"}}
.Ed
.Pp
Its seal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"alg":"ML-DSA-65","context":"noqtis-capsule-1",
"format":"noqtis-seal-1","manifest_sha256":"9f322e95...",
"public_key":"1qWycP39...","signature":"..."}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-check 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-seal 1 ,
.Xr nqchk 3 ,
.Xr nqchkt 3 ,
.Xr nqseal 3
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%R RFC 8785
.%T JSON Canonicalization Scheme (JCS)
.%D June 2020
.Re
.Rs
.%R RFC 3161
.%T Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure Time-Stamp Protocol (TSP)
.%D August 2001
.Re
.Rs
.%R RFC 9162
.%T Certificate Transparency Version 2.0
.%D December 2021
.Re
.Rs
.%R RFC 3339
.%T Date and Time on the Internet: Timestamps
.%D July 2002
.Re
.Rs
.%R RFC 4648
.%T The Base16, Base32, and Base64 Data Encodings
.%D October 2006
.Re
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The signature's context is always
.Ql noqtis-capsule-1 ,
and a checker refuses any other, so a signature its key holder made for
another purpose over the same bytes is never a seal.
.Pp
A timestamp's TSA certificate path is checked at the token's genTime, and
revocation is not checked: a timestamp is only as good as the TSA's key
was at that time.
If the key is stolen later, tokens with an earlier genTime can be made
that still check; pin the TSAs you trust, and stop trusting every token
of a TSA that announces a key compromise.
